{"search_result_groups":[{"search_result_type":"webResults","items":[{"index":null,"title":"Routes · Undertale | Game Design Lab","snippets":["Routes · Undertale | Game Design Lab Undertale / Game design / Routes Full-game spoilers. This research discusses encounters, characters and endings. Return to Overview Look up the reference 06 Routes Contents Routes Every playthrough resolves to exactly one of three routes, tabled in full in the routes and endings reference. Which one a save gets is decided by two systems the player metrics section keeps separate (the visible EXP/LV pair, and the per-region kill counters and hidden Murder Level that gate Genocide specifically) so a run can carry a high LV from killing without having met a single Genocide requirement. True Pacifist Gated behind a completed Neutral ending, so it cannot be reached on a first playthrough. Its requirement is zero EXP for the entire run (not merely \"spare everything,\" since any kill anywhere disqualifies it) plus the eleven-step friendship chain: Toriel spared, Papyrus's date completed, Undyne's cooking lesson, Mettaton EX talked down by rating rather than fought, and Alphys's letter leading to the True Lab and ... a permanent flag: Chara's ERASE/DO NOT choice both lead to the same reset, and accepting Chara's offer to restore the world alters every subsequent True Pacifist and Genocide ending on that save, detailed in the reference's soulless-routes table. Waterfall's text gate on the path to the bridge: it fires only while the region's kill counter is still short, one of the route's few explicit warnings before an early approach permanently aborts it. A run can also fall short of Genocide without reverting cleanly to a default Neutral ending: aborting partway leaves the kill counters and Murder Level exactly where they stopped, and the Alphys ending exists specifically to catch a Genocide attempt abandoned in Hotland. The full flag ladder behind these routing decisions (Murder Level's 18 save-file steps, the epilogue sub-branches, and every version divergence the wiki records) is worked out in the routes and endings reference. Every playthrough resolves to exactly one of three..."],"semantic_document":null,"url":"https://teemo.dev/game-design/undertale/systems/overview-routes/","last_updated_date":null,"crawled_at":"2026-10-31T00:22:00.0000000Z","language":"en","raw_data":null,"safety_signal":{"is_adult":false}},{"index":null,"title":"Undertale Routes Explained — Neutral, Pacifist \u0026 Genocide","snippets":["Undertale Routes Explained — Neutral, Pacifist \u0026 Genocide UNDERTALE / ROUTES \u0026 ENDINGS Three route families. Many consequences between them. Neutral, True Pacifist, and Genocide are useful names for Undertale’s broad route structure. They are not three equal menu choices: each follows different conditions, remembers different actions, and leaves different consequences. Written and reviewed by Masling Review status Last reviewed: August 23, 2026 Research, sources, and corrections Explore how you approach choices → Major ending-spoiler note This guide explains route conditions, late-game areas, the Barrier, and persistent consequences across later playthroughs. It is a route map, not a spoiler-free recommendation or step-by-step walkthrough. The three commonly discussed Undertale routes Route family Broad condition What makes it distinct Neutral A completed run that does not satisfy all True Pacifist or Genocide conditions. Many ending variants respond to who was killed, spared, or befriended. True Pacifist No EXP or LV gain, required friendships, and the later story sequence unlocked after an ... “wrong” misses the point: the game deliberately makes later knowledge and return part of the structure. A violent run is not automatically Genocide Killing many enemies or choosing little mercy can still lead to a Neutral ending. Genocide requires systematic area-by-area exhaustion and specific boss outcomes. Fans sometimes use “No Mercy” more loosely, but it should not replace the stricter route definition. The distinction matters when reading guides, discussing endings, or interpreting how the game reacts to player intent. Routes are consequences, not personality diagnoses A chosen route may reflect curiosity, completionism, role-play, challenge seeking, or a desire to see alternate writing. SoulVirtues.com does not infer someone’s Kindness, Justice, or other real-world score from the route they played. For the game’s color mechanics rather than its ending structure, read the SOUL mode guide. Sources The..."],"semantic_document":null,"url":"https://soulvirtues.com/undertale/routes","last_updated_date":"2026-08-23T00:00:00.0000000","crawled_at":"2026-11-06T01:26:00.0000000Z","language":"en","raw_data":null,"safety_signal":{"is_adult":false}}]}],"error_message":null,"no_results_message":null,"cached":false}

